tal crash in  april off the coast of greece  the military says it's still  investigating the exact cause of that  crash  mechanical failure has been ruled out  based on the flight data recorder it  says during a complex maneuver to land  on the frigate  the flight director's inputs conflicted  with the aircraft's  autopilot settings and that caused the  helicopter to respond in a way  the crew was not expecting the  helicopter slammed into the sea  the military says it's taken steps to  prevent future issues with the software  including  updating its training manual it plans to  have its cyclone fleet  back in service in the coming days i  assure you  this decision was not taken lightly i  have full confidence  in our personnel at 12 wing share water  and in their  ability to resume flying activities in a  safe manner  all six canadian service members on  board were killed in that crash on april  29th  it was the deadliest day for the  canadian armed forces in more than a  decade  a canadian fitness icon on the record  about racism  coming up how hal johnson turned  discrimination into  inspiration for body break  the prime minister is working hard to  win at canada a seat on the u.n security  council and  the campaigning is intense justin  trudeau spent a lot of time  on the phone today with leaders in india  spain ethiopia and elsewhere  our chief political correspondent david  aiken joins me david what is the focus  of his pitch  well the pitch is as much you'd expect  donna canada is a strong team player  well connected as a member of the g7 the  g20 the commonwealth  the francophone e but since the coven 19  pandemic hit  there has been a new twist to canada's  pitch  canada is now selling itself as a  wealthy country  with the resources expertise and  willingness to help smaller countries  with their health and economic  challenges  as we look at the kind of world we're  going to come out to come out of  post covet we need a country like canada  that is  big enough to make a difference but  small enough to know we can't do it  alone and we will continue to work  together  but canada has some challenges of its  own to overcome first of all  its rivals norway and ireland have been  campaigning for a decade  canada just started when trudeau got  elected in 2015  and every time a liberal politician here  be it trudeau or anyone else  tried to take a dig at the previous  harper government by saying  canada is back well norway would pipe up  and say  norway never left so canada's  inconstancy donna has been something  that its rivals have really been playing  up  david how likely does it appear then  that canada will get the votes it needs  to win a seat  well put it this way members of the  canadian delegation at the u.n in new  york tell me  they are quote cautiously optimistic now  that said  this is a secret ballot and anything can  happen consider this  in 2010 canada had written  assurances from 135 countries that they  would all vote for  canada in writing and on that first  ballot only 114  kept their word and this was what  happens all the time with these votes  that some countries we don't know which  ones say one thing  and then do another so we will see and  of course we lost that 2010 vote  now voting in new york begins at 9 00  a.m  wednesday insiders tell me norway  is likely leading the pack with canada  nipping at ireland's heels  the u.n says they expect first ballot  results  by about 4 pm eastern donna all right  david aiken in ottawa  thanks the u.n security council was  created after the second world war and  its primary responsibility is  maintaining world peace  it is the un's most powerful body and  membership is coveted  here's how it works there are 15 seats  on the council  five belong to permanent members china  france  russia the uk and the u.s the 10  remaining non-permanent seats  have two-year terms and they're  distributed on a regional basis  five seats are reserved for african and  asian nations  there are two seats for latin america  one for eastern europe  and two for western europe and other  countries including canada  this year canada is competing with  norway and ireland for the two open  spots  to win a country must receive two-thirds  of all votes cast for the seat  that means canada must get at least 128  votes from the 193  u.n members casting ballots multiple  rounds of voting may be needed  to reach that threshold  the steroid that's showing promise in  the pandemic  up next who it helps  there is still no vaccine and no  treatment for covid19 but today the uk  government did approve the use of a drug  to treat people who are  severely ill with covid19 it's an  existing steroid that is cheap and  widely available  it is not a cure and it doesn't work on  every patient but as crystal commansing  explains it's offering some hope where  until now  there's been none this steroid used to  treat inflammation  could soon be used around the world to  try and save  incredibly sick covid19 patients  it really is showing quite a significant  effect and it's really important because  the drug itself  is very widely available it's on almost  every pharmacy shelf in every hospital  one of the big challenges with covid19  is trying to stop the body's immune  system from going into overdrive and  attacking itself  a so-called cytokine storm in a trial  led by oxford university  2 000 patients requiring respiratory  support and given  dexamethasone or compared to more than  four thousand  treated without it it's not a drug that  you would use  in the community or in outpatients or in  patients on the wall who didn't have  breathing difficulties  according to the findings dexamethasone  helped prevent the death of  one in every eight covid19 patients on  ventilators  for patients being treated with oxygen  the drug prevented one death in every 25  patients treated  i'm quite honestly elated i thought that  our colleagues in united kingdom did a  fantastic job  uh the top line results were certainly  very impressive dr matthew chang  anticipates the drug will now be used  in canada this is the first medicine  that's been shown to reduce death  in any group with covid and that's  the start of something so it shows this  is possible to do  and as other drugs get looked at the  hope is that they'll add on top of that  hope is exactly what's needed more than  8 million people have been infected  worldwide with more than 400 thousand  deaths so far  crystal gomansen global news london  north korea has sent an explosive  message to its southern counterpart  and the world the north blew up a  building known as the joint liaison  office where officials from north and  south korea had recently worked side by  side  the building has reportedly been vacant  since january because of the pandemic  it opened in 2018 and allowed the first  full-time person-to-person contact  between the koreas  which have technically been at war for  decades relations have deteriorated  over the past year next  the back story on body break and it has  nothing to do  with fitness  a calls for racial justice in the united  states and here in canada have sparked  important conversations about racism  hal johnson and his wife joanne are well  known from participation's body break  he has encouraged canadians to get up  and get fit for more than 30 years  now he's telling the story of how his tv  career began  you think that body break was started  because of fitness well it wasn't  it was started to combat racism  body break became an institution on  canadian television but johnson  explained the idea for the exercise  segment came up  after he got hired for a job at tsn in  april of 1988.  hours later someone higher up in the  company said  no to putting him on air because they  already had  a black sports caster after a second  racist  incident months later johnson says he  sat down to figure out a way  to bring people together and i thought  how can i  how can i change things how can i make  that we can all live work and play  together and there won't be  this attitude that that white and black  and  asian and persons with disabilities and  male female we all can't be together  so i came up with this idea and the idea  is body break  today tsn apologized for what it calls a  shameful part of its past  the network thanked johnson for sharing  his story as a reminder of the impact of  racism in canadian media that continues  today in its statement the network said  we've recognized that even 30 years  later there is still much work to do to  improve our commitment  to on-air and editorial diversity  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen  we leave you tonight with a centenarian  who is retiring  his name is diego and he's a giant  galapagos tortoise who also happens to  have a giant  libido thanks to a breeding program  diego has about 800  offspring and is credited with almost  single-handedly saving his  once-threatened species  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow  [Music]  bye-bye  you
 

